\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textcolor {black}{Three views of the interface. (a) In the full network, infection can cross only through the shown physical edges and their bridge nodes. (b) The two-scale simulator keeps a separate local simulator for each community. The macro layer receives community counts, uses $W_{ij}$ to generate a cross-community infection, and places it on a uniformly selected susceptible node. (c) The ideal two-scale process is equivalent to a surrogate network in which every ordered node pair across the two communities has a weak connection. The connections together have total weight $W_{ij}$. Orange nodes are infected and blue nodes are susceptible.}}}{5}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:schematic}{{1}{5}{Aggregate interface}{figure.1}{}}
